WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.360
You probably pay for more AI than you can

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.420
use. Claude on one machine,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.680
Codex on another, maybe Grok or Copilot

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.860
too. SpecWithCode turns all of it into

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.620
one team.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.030
Start with the basics.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.690
Every machine signs in with the official

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.170
tools, and one machine can hold several.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.350
My M4 Max runs Claude and Codex on the EasyChamp

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.790
admin accounts. The M1 runs Claude and Codex

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:28.930
on the marketing accounts.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.730
Nothing is resold.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:32.980
Only have one subscription?

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.700
Then choose the machine that hosts it with

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.200
enough memory and free disk space because

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.380
every session runs right there on your machine,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.040
not in someone else's cloud.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.550
Every machine runs its own SpecWithCode

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.250
server. You connect them privately over

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.990
your network, an SSH tunnel,

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.950
or Tailscale. Here,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.170
the M4 Max sees the M1 and the M3,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.450
and each one is paired on purpose.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.700
Then, SpecWithCode watches the limits.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.580
Session and weekly usage,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.760
banked resets, and credits for every account.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.320
Pool accounts behind a CLIProxyAPI hub,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.360
and they show up too.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.320
When one runs dry, a thread waits for the

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.040
reset or moves to another provider or machine.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.430
Now the part that changes how you work.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.410
In a project, you talk to one coordinator.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.530
It plans, then starts threads for the work,

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.050
each on the machine and model that fits.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.560
This is EasyChamp, a real project.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.260
One thread is shipping reusable tactical

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.260
templates. Another built club finance and

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.320
is waiting for my review.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.520
Others ran independent reviews.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.000
Each one opens its own pull request.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.170
Open a thread to see exactly what it ran

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.610
and what it is waiting on.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.470
Reply and your message goes through the

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.650
coordinator with the thread as context.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.340
And done is not the same as verified.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.640
You can check the work yourself or let auto

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.700
resolve do it. A thread closes itself only

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.760
when every pull request is merged,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.460
checks pass, and the review is approved.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.720
Anything uncertain waits for you.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.610
Which machines? Linux makes the fastest

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.390
workers, and Windows is a solid worker too.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.270
A Mac works, but APFS is slow for agent

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.670
workloads. So let the Mac orchestrate.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.230
Chrome and marketing tools,

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.430
iOS builds, and computer use.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.480
For a worker, aim for eight cores,

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.280
32 gigabytes of memory,

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.180
and a fast SSD with a few hundred gigabytes

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.220
free. Budget about four gigabytes of memory

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.820
and 10 of disk for every agent running in

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:41.160
parallel.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.510
Your machines, your subscriptions,

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.650
one control desk. SpecWithCode,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.710
open source at specwithcode .dev.
